{"chunk_id": "6e97d1cdceb9", "text": "Yudhishthira warns Sakuni that deceitful gambling is sinful, citing the sage Devala and arguing that honorable men seek victory openly, not through cunning. Sakuni counters with a pragmatic argument: in every contest the skilled challenge the unskilled for the sake of victory, and such motives are accepted. Pressed by the assembly and bound by his vow not to refuse a summons, Yudhishthira reluctantly agrees, attributing events to destiny. Duryodhana announces that Sakuni will cast the dice on his behalf, despite Yudhishthira\u2019s objection that gambling through another is improper. The royal court gathers, uneasy figures like Bhishma, Drona, and Vidura watching with heavy hearts as the match begins. Sakuni immediately wins the first stakes, and Yudhishthira\u2014despite suspecting unfair play\u2014continues, escalating the wagers and setting the tragic course of the dice game.\nYudhishthira reluctantly begins the dice game with Sakuni, who quickly wins the first stakes while the court watches uneasily.\nThemes: gambling and deceit, fate versus choice, moral hesitation, courtly tension, manipulation by Sakuni", "vector": [0.005269354674965143, -0.01550928596407175, 0.00562924612313509, 0.0138592179864645, -0.04028068482875824, 0.018660034984350204, -0.04139431193470955, 0.007150297053158283, -0.01970575749874115, -0.03699412941932678, 0.03145315870642662, -0.0016721374122425914, 0.03454958274960518, 0.004277955275028944, -0.0074694459326565266, 0.0015677348710596561, 0.04267090931534767, -0.004128566477447748, 0.044191960245370865, -0.017193306237459183, -0.022503403946757317, -0.010375740006566048, -0.003826393513008952, 0.009207789786159992, -0.026903586462140083, 0.03435945138335228, 0.014300594106316566, -0.016134003177285194, 0.0018418974941596389, -0.013648714870214462, -0.010199189186096191, -0.007238572463393211, 0.02513808198273182, -0.0077682239934802055, -0.021525586023926735, 0.013614763505756855, -0.005741287954151